      "comment": "Initialize and allocate memory pool for patch fog rendering system.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Check if global memory buffer is already allocated\n2. If not allocated, read pool size from global configuration (default 100)\n3. Calculate required memory: (pool_size + 2) * 0x17cc bytes per entry\n4. Allocate memory using VirtualAlloc with MEM_COMMIT (0x1000) and PAGE_READWRITE (4)\n5. Log allocation event to debug system with pool parameters\n6. Validate allocation succeeded, return 0 if failed\n7. Initialize allocated memory to zero using optimized DWORD clearing loop\n8. Clear any remaining bytes with byte-level loop for partial DWORD alignment\n9. Return 1 indicating successful initialization\n\nParameters:\nNone - Function operates on global state variables\n\nReturns:\n1 (SUCCESS) - Pool successfully initialized and memory allocated\n0 (FAILURE) - Memory allocation failed, pool remains uninitialized\n\nSpecial Cases:\nPool size defaults to 100 entries if configuration value is zero or uninitialized\nMemory is allocated as committed pages with read/write access\nZero initialization ensures clean state for fog rendering calculations\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n0x17cc (6092) - Size in bytes of each fog pool entry/structure\n0x5f3 (1523) - DWORD count calculation factor (0x17cc / 4 = 0x5f3)\n0x3fffffff - Mask to ensure loop counter stays in valid range\n0x1000 - MEM_COMMIT flag for VirtualAlloc\n4 - PAGE_READWRITE protection flag for VirtualAlloc\n\nError Handling:\nVirtualAlloc failure returns NULL, function propagates error with return 0\nNo cleanup required on failure as no resources were successfully allocated\nGlobal buffer pointer remains NULL to allow retry on subsequent calls",
