      "comment": "Converts ASCII string to lowercase in-place by transforming uppercase characters.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Check if input string pointer is NULL and return NULL if so\n2. Load first character from string into current character register (BL)\n3. Initialize string cursor pointer to input string position\n4. While current character is not null terminator:\n   a. Check character classification for uppercase using _isctype or direct table lookup\n   b. Determine classification method based on __mb_cur_max_exref value\n   c. If __mb_cur_max < 2, use direct character type table lookup from _pctype_exref\n   d. Otherwise call _isctype function with character and flag 0x1 (uppercase test)\n   e. If character is uppercase, add 0x20 to convert to lowercase ASCII\n   f. Store converted character back to current position in string\n   g. Advance cursor to next character position\n   h. Load next character and continue loop\n5. Return pointer to original string (now modified in-place)\n\nParameters:\npszString (char *) - Null-terminated ASCII string to convert to lowercase\n\nReturns:\nchar * - Pointer to the same string (modified in-place), or NULL if input was NULL\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- NULL input returns NULL immediately without processing\n- Empty string (first character is null) returns immediately \n- Non-ASCII characters (>127) may have undefined behavior depending on locale\n- Function modifies string in-place, requiring writable memory\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n0x20 (32) - ASCII offset between uppercase and lowercase letters (A=65, a=97, diff=32)\n0x1 - Character classification flag for uppercase test in _isctype\n0x1 - Bitmask for uppercase bit in character type table\n0x2 - Stride for character type table entries (2 bytes per character)\n\nError Handling:\n- No explicit error handling beyond NULL pointer check\n- Relies on _isctype for character classification validation\n- Assumes valid null-terminated string input",
