      "comment": "Finds the length of a null-terminated string within a bounded buffer.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Initialize loop counter to 0\n2. Validate maximum length is positive, return -1 if not\n3. Loop through buffer bytes up to maximum length:\n   a. Check if current byte is null terminator (0x00)\n   b. If null found, return current position (string length)\n   c. Increment counter and continue\n4. If loop completes without finding null, return -1 (not found)\n\nParameters:\nlpszBuffer (char *): Pointer to buffer containing potential null-terminated string\nnMaxLength (int): Maximum number of bytes to search before giving up\n\nReturns:\n>= 0: Position of first null terminator (effective string length)\n-1: No null terminator found within nMaxLength bytes\n\nSpecial Cases:\nnMaxLength <= 0: Returns -1 immediately without buffer access\nEmpty string (first byte null): Returns 0\nBuffer contains no nulls: Returns -1 after checking nMaxLength bytes\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n0x00: Null terminator character marking end of string\n-1 (0xFFFFFFFF): Error return value indicating string not null-terminated within limit",
